  Implement a clear, deterministic “last-seen player army” snapshot in main.py’s EnemyAI.

  Requirements:
  - Reuse the existing fog-safe observation system in EnemyAI._update_strategic_knowledge().
  - The AI must never inspect hidden player units directly.
  - Store the most recently observed player squad/army as snapshots containing at least:
    - uid
    - unit kind
    - last observed health
    - observation time
  - Update a unit’s snapshot when it is actually visible to a red unit or the enemy keep.
  - Remove a unit from the snapshot when the AI visibly confirms that it died.
  - Do not erase the last-seen army merely because the ordinary PLAYER_KNOWLEDGE_TTL expires. The attack-
  launch rule in a later step must be able to compare against the player squad that was last seen.
  - Keep short-lived tactical observations separate from this persistent strategic snapshot so existing
  combat retreat behavior can still distinguish fresh and stale information.
  - Add a helper that returns the last-seen player composition both as unit counts and essence totals
  using UNIT_COSTS.
  - Preserve deterministic ordering.

  Add tests covering:
  1. Hidden distant player units never enter the snapshot.
  2. Visible player units enter it with the correct kind, health, and timestamp.
  3. A visible update replaces health/position facts as appropriate.
  4. A visibly confirmed death removes the unit.
  5. Losing vision does not immediately erase the last-seen snapshot.
  6. Essence totals use UNIT_COSTS, not unit counts.

  Checks:
  - Run the focused observation/knowledge tests.
  - Run: python3 -m unittest discover -s tests
  - Fix all regressions before stopping.
  - Briefly report the data model added and the exact test results.
